from svgkit import *
from hub_figs import WIDE
from ip_address import tool_html,tool_js   # the same live check as the IP address page, imported so the two cannot drift apart
F1,F2,F3,F4,F5=WIDE
S=dict(slug='how-to-find-ip-address-of-any-device',dir='guides',date='2026-06-29',date_label='',plan='A1',tool_html=tool_html,tool_js=tool_js,
title="How to Find an IP Address on Any Device or Site",headline="How to Find an IP Address: Your Own, Another Device, a Website or a Server",
desc="Find your public IP with a live check, your device's IP on Windows, Mac, Linux, iPhone and Android, your router's, another device's or a website's.",
kw=["how to find ip address","how to find my ip address","how do i find my ip address","how to check ip address","how to find ip address on iphone","how to find ip address of a device","how to find ip address of a website","how to find ip address on windows","find ip address of any device"],
about='Finding an IP address',section='Network Guides',crumb='How to Find an IP Address',
h1='How to Find an <span class="ndic-highlight">IP Address</span>',tagline="Your public address, your device's, your router's, another device's or a website's: pick the case and follow the steps, with a live check of your own address.",kicker='Network Guide',cta='Check My IP',
qa_title="Which IP address do you need?",
qa_body="To find your public IP address, use the live check on this page. To find your device's own address, run ipconfig on Windows or open the Wi-Fi details on Mac, Linux, iPhone and Android. For another device, read your router's device list. For a website, run nslookup or dig. The right method depends on which address you mean.",
og_title='How to Find an <span style="color:#38bdf8">IP Address</span>',og_sub="Yours, a device's, your router's or a website's",og_chip='192 . 168 . 1 . <span style="color:#38bdf8">42</span>',
card_desc="How to find an IP address: your public IP with a live check, your device on Windows, Mac, Linux, iPhone and Android, your router, another device or a website.",
panel=dict(eyebrow='Network Identity panel',h3='See the full picture of your address',p='The panel adds ISP, ASN, connection type, VPN and hosting flags and a map. For the path your connection takes to the internet, open the <a href="/intelligence/network-route">Network Route panel</a>.',href='/intelligence/network-identity',btn='Open Network Identity'),
sections=[
('check-yours','Find Your Public IP Address Right Now','''<p>The address websites see is your public IP address. The live check above shows it, with your ISP and an approximate location. Other ways to see it: search for "what is my IP" on Google, which shows it at the top of the results; run <code class="ir-code">curl ifconfig.me</code> in a terminal, which prints it on one line; or on Windows run <code class="ir-code">nslookup myip.opendns.com resolver1.opendns.com</code>. The last two ask an outside service, so they need a connection.</p><p>If you need a different address, start from the case below.</p>'''+fig(F1,'Pick the address you need, then follow the section for it.')+'''<p>Your home has one public address shared by every device, and each device has its own local address. <a href="/learn/what-is-an-ip-address">What Is an IP Address?</a> explains the difference in full.</p>'''),
('own-device',"Find Your Device's Own IP Address",'''<p>Your device's own address is a local address, the one your router gave it, usually starting 192.168 or 10. It is different from the public address above, which belongs to the whole home network.</p>'''+table("Where to find your device's IP address",['Device','Where to look','What you see'],[['Windows 10 and 11','Settings, Network &amp; internet, your Wi-Fi or Ethernet connection, Properties, or run ipconfig in Command Prompt','IPv4 address'],['Mac','System Settings, Network, your connection, Details, TCP/IP','IP address'],['Linux','Run ip addr or hostname -I in a terminal','The inet address of your adapter'],['iPhone or iPad','Settings, Wi-Fi, the info icon next to your network','IP Address'],['Android','Settings, Network &amp; internet, Wi-Fi, tap your network (some phones need Advanced)','IP address'],['Chromebook','Select the time, then your Wi-Fi network, then Network','IP address']])+fig(F2,'A home has one public address on the router; every device has its own local address.')+table('Other addresses on the same screen',['Address','What it is','Where to read it'],[['Gateway or Router',"Your router's local address, the one that opens its settings page",'ipconfig shows Default Gateway; Mac and iPhone show Router'],['DNS servers','The servers your device asks to turn names into addresses','ipconfig /all on Windows; the DNS tab in Network Details on Mac']])+'''<p>If pages start slowly even though the connection is fast, the DNS servers may be the cause: see <a href="/learn/how-to-fix-slow-dns-lookup">how to fix a slow DNS lookup</a>.</p>'''),
('router',"Find Your Router's IP Address",'''<p>Your router's local address is the default gateway that your devices send traffic to. On Windows, run <code class="ir-code">ipconfig</code> and read the Default Gateway line. On a Mac, open Network Details and read Router. On an iPhone, open the info icon next to your Wi-Fi network and read Router. On Android, tap your network and look for Gateway, sometimes under Advanced. Type it into a browser's address bar to open the router's settings page.</p><p>Our <a href="/learn/find-router-ip-address">guide to finding your router's IP address</a> shows which ipconfig lines to read and what to do when the page will not open.</p>'''),
('other-device','Find the IP Address of Another Device on Your Network','''<p>The router handed every device its address, so the router's device list is the most complete place to look. Open your router's page, find <strong>Connected devices</strong>, <strong>DHCP clients</strong> or <strong>Device list</strong>, and match the device by name or manufacturer. The address is on the same row.</p>'''+fig(F3,'The router list sees every device it gave an address to; the other methods see only part of the network.')+table('Three ways to list devices',['Method','What it shows','Limit'],[['Router device list','Every device the router gave an address to','Needs the router login'],['arp -a on Windows','Devices this computer has recently talked to (its ARP cache)','A device it has not contacted will not appear; ping it first'],['Scanner app, such as Fing or Advanced IP Scanner','Devices that answer a scan of the network range','Needs an install; a device that ignores the scan will not appear']])+'''<p><strong>Printers.</strong> A printer shows its address on its own screen or on a printed network page, and Windows keeps it in the printer's port. Our <a href="/learn/find-printer-ip-address">printer IP guide</a> covers Brother, HP, Epson and Canon.</p><p><strong>Roku.</strong> With a remote, open Settings, then Network, then About. Without one, the Roku mobile app on the same Wi-Fi shows the same details under Devices, or you can read the Roku's row in the router's device list.</p>'''),
('website','Find the IP Address of a Website or Game Server','''<p>A site name is turned into an address by DNS, and you can ask for the answer directly. On Windows, run <code class="ir-code">nslookup example.com</code> and read the Address lines under the answer. On a Mac or Linux, run <code class="ir-code">dig +short example.com</code>. <code class="ir-code">ping example.com</code> also prints the address (<code class="ir-code">ping -n 1</code> on Windows, <code class="ir-code">ping -c 1</code> on Mac and Linux).</p>'''+fig(F4,'Your computer asks a resolver, the resolver asks the site, and the answer comes back as an address.')+'''<p>For a game server shown as <code class="ir-code">play.example.com:25565</code>, use only the name before the colon, because the number after it is the port, not part of the address. Large sites can return several addresses, and the one you get can depend on where you ask from, so the result is an address you were given, not the only one.</p>'''),
('someone-else',"Find Someone Else's IP Address",'''<p>You cannot look up a stranger's address from outside. You can only see an address that contacted you: your own web server's logs show the addresses of visitors, and the full headers of an email show the servers that handled it. Either one points to an ISP or a mail server and an approximate region, not to a person or a street address, as <a href="/learn/what-is-an-ip-address#reveals">what an IP address reveals</a> explains.</p><p>Do not try to track individuals from an address. For harassment or threats, report them to the platform or the police, who can ask the ISP for the account details.</p>'''),
('why-different','Why the Address You Find May Not Be the One You Expect','''<p>Two tools can report different public addresses for the same home, and neither need be wrong. A VPN changes the address websites see: with it on, the live check shows the VPN server's address, not your ISP's. The <a href="/intelligence/vpn-proxy">VPN and Proxy panel</a> flags this.</p>'''+fig(F5,'With a VPN on, websites see the VPN server address instead of yours.')+table('Why addresses differ',['Situation','What happens','What to do'],[['A VPN is on','Websites see the VPN server address','Turn it off to see your ISP address'],['Your router shows a different public address than the live check','Your ISP may share one address between many customers (carrier-grade NAT, the 100.64.0.0/10 range)','Ask your ISP, or read the explanation in What Is an IP Address?'],['Wi-Fi and mobile data give different addresses','They are different networks with different public addresses','Check on the network you actually use'],['Your address changed','Most public addresses and all DHCP local addresses are dynamic','Reserve a local address on the router if a device needs a fixed one']])+'''<p>A VPN can still leak your real address through WebRTC. The <a href="/learn/webrtc-leak-test-and-shield">WebRTC leak test</a> checks for it.</p>'''),
('reference','Quick Reference: Which Method for Which Case','''<p>Every common case, mapped to the fastest method.</p>'''+table('Find an IP address: quick reference',['What you need','Fastest method','Where'],[['Your public IP','The live check on this page','Browser'],['Your public IP from a terminal','curl ifconfig.me','Terminal'],["Your device's IP on Windows",'ipconfig','Command Prompt'],["Your device's IP on Mac, Linux or phone",'The network details for your connection','Settings, or ip addr on Linux'],["Your router's IP",'The default gateway','ipconfig or the Wi-Fi details'],['A printer','The printer screen or a network page','Printer menu'],['Any device at home','The router device list','Browser, at the gateway address'],['A Roku','Settings, Network, About','Roku remote or app'],['A website or server','nslookup or dig','Terminal']]))],
logic="Start from which address the reader needs, because the same question has different answers: the address websites see, the address of this device, the router, another device or a website. Each section answers one case and links to the guide that goes deeper.",
method="Commands and menu paths follow Microsoft documentation for ipconfig and arp, the macOS dig manual, and TP-Link support pages for Windows, Mac, Chromebook and phones. The terminal methods for your public address come from independent guides (DigiCert, Avast, GeeksforGeeks) and depend on outside services. The Roku route is confirmed by several independent guides, not by Roku's own page. The Microsoft nslookup page was carried over from the previous version and not reopened. That large sites can return several addresses depending on where you ask is general DNS behaviour, not tied to a cited page. Menu names vary by version and maker, and steps were not run on every device.",
cites=[("Microsoft Learn: ipconfig",'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/ipconfig'),("Microsoft Learn: arp",'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/arp'),("Microsoft Learn: nslookup",'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/nslookup'),("macOS manual page: dig",'https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/dig.1.html'),("TP-Link: find your IP address on Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android and more",'https://www.tp-link.com/support/faq/838/'),("TP-Link: find the IP address of an Android phone, iPhone or iPad",'https://tp-link.com/support/faq/2375/'),("DigiCert: how can I find out my IP address",'https://www.digicert.com/blog/how-can-i-find-out-my-ip-address'),("Avast: how to find your IP address",'https://www.avast.com/c-how-to-find-ip-address'),("GeeksforGeeks: how to find your IP address",'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/techtips/how-to-find-your-ip-address/'),("How-To Geek: how to find your Roku IP address",'https://www.howtogeek.com/806547/how-to-find-roku-ip-address/'),("Private Internet Access: how to find your Roku IP address",'https://www.privateinternetaccess.com/blog/how-to-find-roku-ip-address/'),("Apple Support: change DNS settings on Mac",'https://support.apple.com/en-gb/guide/mac-help/mh14127'),("RFC 1918: Address Allocation for Private Internets",'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918'),("RFC 6598: IANA-Reserved IPv4 Prefix for Shared Address Space",'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6598')],
faq_sub='Fourteen questions about finding an IP address.',
faq=[("How do I find my IP address?","Use the live check on this page for your public address, the one websites see. For your device's own address, run ipconfig on Windows or open the Wi-Fi or Network details on Mac, Linux, iPhone and Android. They are different addresses, so check which one you need."),
("How do I find my IP address on my phone?","On an iPhone, open Settings, then Wi-Fi, and tap the info icon next to your network: the IP Address line is your phone's local address. On Android, open Settings, Network &amp; internet, Wi-Fi, tap your network and look for IP address, which sometimes sits under Advanced. Menus vary by maker. To see the public address your phone uses, open the live check on this page."),
("What is the difference between a local IP and a public IP address?","A local IP is assigned by your router and only works inside your home network, typically 192.168.x.x or 10.x.x.x. A public IP is assigned by your ISP and is the address the internet sees when any device on your network goes online. All devices on one home network share it."),
("How do I find the IP address of my router?","Run ipconfig on Windows and read Default Gateway, or open Network Details on a Mac and read Router, or tap the info icon next to your Wi-Fi network on an iPhone and read Router. Typing it into a browser opens the router's page. The address is also often printed on a label on the router."),
("How do I find the IP address of any device on my network?","Open your router's page and read its connected devices or DHCP client list, which shows every device with its address. On Windows, arp -a shows only devices your computer has recently talked to. Scanner apps such as Fing or Advanced IP Scanner list devices that answer a scan."),
("How do I find a printer's IP address?","The printer's own screen or a printed network page shows it, and Windows keeps it in the printer's port. See our <a href=\"/learn/find-printer-ip-address\">guide to finding a printer's IP address</a> for Brother, HP, Epson and Canon steps."),
("How do I find my Roku's IP address?","With a remote, go to Settings, Network, About. Without one, the Roku mobile app on the same Wi-Fi shows the details under Devices, or find the Roku's row in the router's device list."),
("How do I find the IP address of a website?","Run nslookup example.com on Windows or dig +short example.com on Mac and Linux, replacing example.com with the site. The address in the answer is the site's address. Large sites can return several, and the answer can differ by location."),
("What command finds the IP address of a Minecraft server?","Run nslookup followed by the server's host name, for example nslookup play.example.com. If the name is shown with a port, such as play.example.com:25565, use only the part before the colon. ping play.example.com -n 1 also shows the address in square brackets. Server addresses can change if the operator moves the server."),
("Why does arp -a not show every device?","The ARP cache only holds devices your computer has recently talked to, so a device you have not contacted will not appear. Ping the device or use the router's device list instead."),
("How do I find my DNS server?","On Windows run ipconfig /all and read DNS Servers. On a Mac open Network Details and the DNS tab. Your router often acts as the DNS server and forwards to your ISP, and you can change it: see <a href=\"/learn/how-to-fix-slow-dns-lookup\">how to fix a slow DNS lookup</a>."),
("Why does my IP address look different on different tools?","A VPN changes the address websites see. Some ISPs also share one public address between customers (carrier-grade NAT), so your router's WAN address can differ from the one the live check shows. Mobile data and Wi-Fi are different networks with different addresses."),
("Can I find someone else's IP address?","Not from outside. You can see the address of something that contacted you, such as a visitor in your server logs or the servers in an email's headers. The address points to an ISP and an approximate region, not to a person."),
("Does my IP address change?","Often yes. Most home public addresses, and all local addresses from DHCP, are dynamic and can change when a lease expires or equipment restarts. A reservation on the router keeps a local address fixed.")])
